\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{The dissociation is not Krum-specific: all four aggregators, all four channels.}{35}{section*.38}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A11}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The four channels, all four aggregators, under Mode~S.} Endpoint change $\kappa {=}0 \to \kappa {=}2$. \textbf  {The first column, $\Delta $~agg., is the relative displacement of the aggregate the defense \emph  {emits}}, $\|\mathrm  {agg}(T(U)) - \mathrm  {agg}(U)\|/\|\mathrm  {agg}(U)\|$: what enters training, and \textbf  {not} the value of a scoring statistic; the measured channels are aggregate displacement, decision, admission and influence. The dissociation between the \emph  {decision} channel and the \emph  {admission} channel is not a property of one aggregator: it holds for a selector, a cosine-invariant selector, a coordinate-wise order statistic and a weighted averager. The table does \textbf  {not} order the arms ($\Delta \Lambda _a$ and $\Delta $ASR disagree in rank, which is §\ref {app:targeted_full}'s refutation stated in one row-pair) and it is \emph  {not} an out-of-sample test: all rows are Mode-S ladders reported elsewhere in this paper. \textbf  {The sixth row is the score-only control}: the same arm as the first with the magnitude channel closed, so its $\Delta $~agg.\ is the re-selection component alone and its decision, admission and influence columns are the first row's own numbers, score-only leaving the selection untouched.}}{36}{table.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:channels_full}{{A11}{36}{\textbf {The four channels, all four aggregators, under Mode~S.} Endpoint change $\kappa {=}0 \to \kappa {=}2$. \textbf {The first column, $\Delta $~agg., is the relative displacement of the aggregate the defense \emph {emits}}, $\|\mathrm {agg}(T(U)) - \mathrm {agg}(U)\|/\|\mathrm {agg}(U)\|$: what enters training, and \textbf {not} the value of a scoring statistic; the measured channels are aggregate displacement, decision, admission and influence. The dissociation between the \emph {decision} channel and the \emph {admission} channel is not a property of one aggregator: it holds for a selector, a cosine-invariant selector, a coordinate-wise order statistic and a weighted averager. The table does \textbf {not} order the arms ($\Delta \Lambda _a$ and $\Delta $ASR disagree in rank, which is §\ref {app:targeted_full}'s refutation stated in one row-pair) and it is \emph {not} an out-of-sample test: all rows are Mode-S ladders reported elsewhere in this paper. \textbf {The sixth row is the score-only control}: the same arm as the first with the magnitude channel closed, so its $\Delta $~agg.\ is the re-selection component alone and its decision, admission and influence columns are the first row's own numbers, score-only leaving the selection untouched}{table.caption.39}{}}
